p-queue v10.0.0. The concrete matching bug is in the default PriorityQueue implementation in source/priority-queue.ts: the lookup predicate used by setPriority is `element.id !== id`, so it finds the first live element whose id is not the target instead of the target element.

paths
source/priority-queue.ts - PriorityQueue implementation and the bug at line 93.
outcome: what
p-queue v10.0.0. `setPriority` exists in two layers: the public `PQueue.setPriority` wrapper (source/index.ts) and the default `PriorityQueue#setPriority` (source/priority-queue.ts). The concrete matching bug is in `PriorityQueue#setPriority`: its lookup predicate is `element.id !== id`, so it selects the first live element whose id is *not* the target instead of the element whose id *is* the target.

paths
source/priority-queue.ts — PriorityQueue implementation (the bug is at line 93).
source/index.ts — PQueue public API and `setPriority` wrapper (lines 837–852).
source/lower-bound.ts — binary insertion helper used by `PriorityQueue#enqueue`.
source/queue.ts — `Queue` interface implemented by PriorityQueue and custom queue classes.
source/options.ts — `QueueAddOptions` (priority/id/timeout/signal etc.).
test/priority-queue.ts — direct PriorityQueue tests, including the failing one around consumed/dequeued matching.
test/validation.ts — PQueue priority/id validation and unknown-id error tests.
test/debug.ts — runningTasks/setPriority and custom queue class behavior tests.
test/basic.ts — PQueue end-to-end setPriority ordering test.
test/advanced.ts — many `.setPriority()` ordering tests.
test/helpers.ts — `FifoQueue` custom queue class used by tests.
package.json — test script.

facts
1. Signatures and methods
source/queue.ts lines 1–10:
```
export type RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>;

export type Queue<Element, Options> = {
	size: number;
	filter: (options: Readonly<Partial<Options>>) => Element[];
	dequeue: () => Element | undefined;
	enqueue: (run: Element, options?: Partial<Options>) => void;
	setPriority: (id: string, priority: number) => void;
	remove: (id: string) => void;
};
```
source/priority-queue.ts:
- line 58: `enqueue(run: RunFunction, options?: Partial<PriorityQueueOptions>): void`
- line 120: `dequeue(): RunFunction | undefined`
- line 92: `setPriority(id: string, priority: number)` (implicitly `void`)
- lines 107–108: `remove(id: string): void; remove(run: RunFunction): void;` (impl lines 109–118)
- line 124: `filter(options: Readonly<Partial<PriorityQueueOptions>>): RunFunction[]`
- line 137: `get size(): number`

source/index.ts:
- line 837: `setPriority(id: string, priority: number)`

2. Internal data structure
source/priority-queue.ts lines 5–16:
```
const compactionThreshold = 100;
...
export default class PriorityQueue implements Queue<RunFunction, PriorityQueueOptions> {
	// The slots before `#head` are `undefined`.
	readonly #queue: Array<PriorityQueueOptions & {run: RunFunction} | undefined> = [];

	// The queue is stored as a sorted array, but dequeued slots are left before `#head` until compaction. Only items from `#head` onward are live, which keeps repeated dequeues amortized O(1).
	#head = 0;
```
It is a plain JS array, kept sorted descending by priority (highest first). Consumed slots before `#head` are left as `undefined`; live range is `#queue[#head] .. #queue[length-1]`.

Queue entry shape is created in `enqueue`, source/priority-queue.ts lines 58–69:
```
	enqueue(run: RunFunction, options?: Partial<PriorityQueueOptions>): void {
		const {
			priority = 0,
			id,
		} = options ?? {};

		const {size} = this;
		const element = {
			priority,
			id,
			run,
		};
```
So each live element is `{priority: number, id: string | undefined, run: RunFunction}`.

3. Exact setPriority lookup
source/priority-queue.ts lines 92–105:
```
	setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
		const index = this.#findIndex(element => element.id !== id);
		if (index === -1) {
			throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
		}

		// Moving it would put it behind the tasks with the same priority.
		if (this.#queue[index]!.priority === priority) {
			return;
		}

		const [item] = this.#queue.splice(index, 1);
		this.enqueue(item!.run, {priority, id});
	}
```
`#findIndex` is a linear scan over the live range only, source/priority-queue.ts lines 28–37:
```
	#findIndex(isMatch: (element: PriorityQueueOptions & {run: RunFunction}) => boolean): number {
		for (let index = this.#head; index < this.#queue.length; index++) {
			if (isMatch(this.#queue[index]!)) {
				return index;
			}
		}

		return -1;
	}
```
The predicate is inverted (`element.id !== id`). Effect: for any non-empty live range where the first live element's id is not the target, it returns that first index; it returns `-1` only if every live element's id equals the target, or if the live range is empty. It then early-returns on the wrong element's priority check, or splices out the wrong `run` and re-enqueues it under the target id and new priority. The real target stays where it was.

`enqueue` uses `lowerBound` for binary insertion only when the new priority is higher than the current tail, source/priority-queue.ts lines 71–90:
```
		if (size === 0) {
			this.#queue.length = 0;
			this.#head = 0;
			this.#queue.push(element);
			return;
		}

		if (this.#queue.at(-1)!.priority! >= priority) {
			this.#queue.push(element);
			return;
		}

		this.#compact();
		const index = lowerBound(this.#queue, element, (a, b) => a!.priority === b!.priority ? 0 : b!.priority! - a!.priority!);
		this.#queue.splice(index, 0, element);
```
`lowerBound` is source/lower-bound.ts lines 3–20 (binary lower bound).

4. Dequeue/poll mechanics
source/priority-queue.ts lines 39–56:
```
	#removeFirst(): RunFunction {
		const item = this.#queue[this.#head]!;
		// Drop the reference, so that the function of the task and what it holds can be garbage collected before compaction.
		this.#queue[this.#head] = undefined;
		this.#head++;

		if (this.#head === this.#queue.length) {
			this.#queue.length = 0;
			this.#head = 0;
		} else if (this.#head > compactionThreshold && this.#head > this.#queue.length / 2) {
			this.#compact();
		}

		return item.run;
	}
```
source/priority-queue.ts lines 120–122:
```
	dequeue(): RunFunction | undefined {
		return this.#head === this.#queue.length ? undefined : this.#removeFirst();
	}
```
Dequeue does not `shift()`/`splice()` the consumed element; it nulls the head slot and advances `#head`. Therefore live elements keep their original array indices after dequeues; ID matching is not broken by dequeues. Only `#compact()` (lines 18–26) physically splices the consumed prefix away:
```
	#compact(): void {
		if (this.#head === 0) {
			return;
		}

		this.#queue.splice(0, this.#head);
		this.#head = 0;
	}
```
It is invoked when head > 100 and head > length/2 in `#removeFirst` (line 50), and before binary insertion in `enqueue` (line 86).

5. Missing/unknown ID behavior
Public `PQueue.setPriority` source/index.ts lines 837–852:
```
	setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
		if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
			throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
		}

		// Checked here, not only in `PriorityQueue`, so that it also throws with a custom queue class.
		for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
			if (waitingTask.id === id) {
				this.#queue.setPriority(id, priority);
				waitingTask.priority = priority;
				return;
			}
		}

		throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
	}
```
`#waitingTasks` is `new Set<WaitingTask>()` at line 154. `WaitingTask` shape is at lines 81–90 (`id?`, `priority?`, etc.). The wrapper uses strict `===` on `waitingTask.id`.

Exact unknown-id error in both layers:
```
ReferenceError: No promise function with the id "<id>" exists in the queue.
```
PriorityQueue itself uses the same message at source/priority-queue.ts line 95. Padding/punctuation matches in tests (see below).

Invalid-priority error in the wrapper (line 839):
```
TypeError: Expected `priority` to be a finite number, got `<value>` (<typeof>)
```
Direct `PriorityQueue#setPriority` does not do finite-number validation; validation is done by `PQueue.setPriority`, and `enqueue`/`#add` reject `NaN` via `#add` lines 626–629.

Because of the inverted predicate, direct `PriorityQueue#setPriority` only throws for an unknown id when the live range is empty or every live element's id equals the target; otherwise it mutates a wrong element.

6. Existing tests
Test command in package.json line 21: `npm test` → `xo && node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/*.ts && del-cli dist && tsc && nz`. Running only node tests is `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/*.ts`.

Direct-priority-queue tests (test/priority-queue.ts):
- lines 16–35 `PriorityQueue ignores dequeued items in queue operations`: after first dequeue, `queue.setPriority('same', 2); queue.remove(third); assert.equal(queue.size, 1); assert.equal(queue.dequeue(), second);`
- lines 119–144 `PriorityQueue ignores consumed items with no live match`: after dequeuing `first`, expects `setPriority('first', 1)` to throw `{name: 'ReferenceError', message: 'No promise function with the id "first" exists in the queue.'}` (lines 129–137). This is the clearest failing test for the inverted predicate, since currently it finds the still-live `second` at `#head` and does not throw.
- lines 171–186 `PriorityQueue setPriority works when only one live item remains`: after dequeuing `first`, `queue.setPriority('second', 1)` is expected not to throw; currently every live element has id `second`, so `#findIndex` returns `-1` and it throws.
- lines 216–249 `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority keeps the position...`: `setPriority('firstHigh', Infinity)`, `setPriority('first', 1)`, `setPriority('firstLow', -Infinity)`, then after partial dequeue more same-priority calls; expected order assertions at 240 and 245–248.
- lines 251–258 `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority still throws for an unknown id`: `assert.throws(() => { queue.setPriority('missing', 1); }, ReferenceError);` with one live item whose id is `first`; currently `#findIndex` returns index 0 and this fails.
- lines 260–279 `PriorityQueue setPriority moves an Infinity priority item behind...`: calls `setPriority('firstHigh', 0)` and `setPriority('low', 1)` and asserts full dequeue order `[secondHigh, low, normal, firstHigh]`.
- lines 351–385 removal/compaction test with `queue.setPriority('299', 2); assert.equal(queue.dequeue(), runs[299]); ...` at lines 371–372.

FIFO-at-equal-priority tests (test/priority-queue.ts):
- lines 188–201 `PriorityQueue keeps FIFO order among Infinity priorities`: asserts `[first, second, third, low]`.
- lines 203–214 `PriorityQueue orders -Infinity priorities last and keeps their FIFO order`: asserts `[normal, first, second]`.
- lines 281–300 `PriorityQueue keeps FIFO order among Infinity priorities inserted after partial dequeue`: asserts `[secondHigh, thirdHigh, second]`.
- lines 59–79 same-priority append after partial dequeue.

Missing-ID/error tests:
- test/priority-queue.ts lines 129–137 and 255–257 (quoted above).
- test/validation.ts lines 248–294 `'setPriority validates input, and a rejected priority keeps the order'`: line 260–264 asserts unknown id error with regex `{message: /No promise function with the id "non-existent" exists in the queue/v}`; lines 272–285 assert TypeError for NaN/Infinity/-Infinity/'1'/undefined; line 288 valid `setPriority('third', 10)`; line 293 asserts order `['first', 'third', 'second']`.
- test/validation.ts lines 296–308 `'setPriority with the current priority still throws for a running task'`: asserts `setPriority('running', 1)` throws `ReferenceError`.
- test/debug.ts lines 351–368: several valid `setPriority` calls then `runningTasks` priority equals `-2`.
- test/debug.ts lines 370–383: running task priority unchanged when `setPriority` throws.
- test/debug.ts lines 407–443: custom `RecordingQueue extends FifoQueue` with `override setPriority(id: string) { calls.push(id); }`; asserts `setPriority` throws `ReferenceError` for `'unknown'`, `'running'`, `'aborted'`, then `queue.setPriority('waiting', 7)` invokes `calls` `['waiting']`.
- test/basic.ts lines 1766–1788: end-to-end PQueue test moving an Infinity-priority task behind same-priority batch, asserts order `['secondHigh', 'thirdHigh', 'normal', 'firstHigh', 'lowest']`.
- test/advanced.ts lines 895–913, 1147–1177, 1179–1286, 1856–1897: many ordering tests for valid `.setPriority()` movement.

verdict
The bug is the predicate at source/priority-queue.ts line 93: `this.#findIndex(element => element.id !== id)` should match `element.id === id` to find the target. Dequeue/compaction mechanics are sound for id matching because dequeues only advance `#head` and null consumed slots, and `#findIndex` starts at `#head`; `#compact` restores the invariant before binary insertion. The public `PQueue.setPriority` wrapper is not the source of this bug, since it first locates the waiting task by strict id equality in `#waitingTasks` and only then delegates; the broken matching is in the default queue class.

reasoning
The fix is a one-predicate change: make `#findIndex` select the live element whose `id` equals the passed id. The surrounding logic (throw on `-1`, same-priority early return at `this.#queue[index]`, splice-and-re-enqueue via `enqueue(item.run, {priority, id})`) is already built around `#head`-relative live-range matching, so no structural change is needed. The wrapper's finite-priority validation stays in PQueue, but the direct PriorityQueue's unknown-id behavior becomes correct once the predicate matches.

test_plan
Run the focused default queue tests first: `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-name-pattern='PriorityQueue' test/priority-queue.ts`. Expected: `PriorityQueue ignores consumed items with no live match`, `PriorityQueue setPriority works when only one live item remains`, `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority keeps the position...`, and `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority still throws for an unknown id` all pass. Then run full queue tests: `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/priority-queue.ts test/validation.ts test/debug.ts test/basic.ts test/advanced.ts`. Expected: validation unknown-id/TypeError cases, runningTasks cases, and the basic/advanced ordering assertions all pass. Both files currently report no TypeScript diagnostics (`No diagnostics for 'source/priority-queue.ts'` and `source/index.ts`), and `xo` should stay clean.

leftover
No environment failures. I did not execute the test suite (tooling here has no shell command execution), so the pass/fail predictions are from reading the predicate and assertions, not from observed test output. Tests already appear written to catch this exact inversion, but whether someone intended `!==` for any other reason is not resolved beyond it contradicting the test expectations.

